WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.839
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.139 --> 00:00:08.537
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:15.286
Unit twenty-six: The Cold War. Today you will make concessions in historical argument with 'although' and 'despite'.

4
00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:18.285
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.698
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.447
Cold War.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:33.115
Cold War means the rivalry between the USA and USSR from 1947 to 1991.

8
00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.633
The Cold War divided the world into two blocs.

9
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.798
Your turn. Say: Cold War.

10
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:43.658
Cold War.

11
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:45.476
Superpower.

12
00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:50.120
Superpower means an extremely powerful and influential state.

13
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.761
Both superpowers built huge nuclear arsenals.

14
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.026
Your turn. Say: Superpower.

15
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:01.100
Superpower.

16
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.025
Ideology.

17
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.084
Ideology means a system of political ideas.

18
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:10.832
Capitalism and communism were rival ideologies.

19
00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:14.406
Your turn. Say: Ideology.

20
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:18.442
Ideology.

21
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:20.289
Arms race.

22
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:25.152
Arms race means competition to build more and better weapons.

23
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.342
The arms race cost billions of dollars.

24
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.113
Your turn. Say: Arms race.

25
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:36.374
Arms race.

26
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:38.164
Alliance.

27
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.485
Alliance means an agreement between countries to support each other.

28
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:45.373
NATO is a military alliance.

29
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:48.670
Your turn. Say: Alliance.

30
00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:52.604
Alliance.

31
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.374
Proxy war.

32
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:59.519
Proxy war means a war in which superpowers support opposing local sides.

33
00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:01.977
Vietnam became a proxy war.

34
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:05.294
Your turn. Say: Proxy war.

35
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.309
Proxy war.

36
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.121
Diplomacy.

37
00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:15.834
Diplomacy means managing international relations by negotiation.

38
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.597
Diplomacy prevented a nuclear war in 1962.

39
00:02:21.183 --> 00:02:24.033
Your turn. Say: Diplomacy.

40
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:27.949
Diplomacy.

41
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:29.650
Détente.

42
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.054
Détente means a period of easing tension between rivals.

43
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.066
Détente in the 1970s reduced the risk of war.

44
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.871
Your turn. Say: Détente.

45
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:45.700
Détente.

46
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.942
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:55.108
Visal, people say the Cold War was 'cold'. Was it really peaceful?

48
00:02:56.217 --> 00:03:06.346
Only for the superpowers. Despite the arms race, direct war between the USA and USSR was avoided, but millions died in proxy wars.

49
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.299
How was Cambodia affected?

50
00:03:10.383 --> 00:03:22.893
Severely. Although Cambodia tried to stay neutral in the 1960s, it was drawn into the Vietnam War, and US bombing devastated the countryside.

51
00:03:23.983 --> 00:03:25.797
What lesson do you take from this?

52
00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:35.756
In spite of their size, small nations' fates were often decided by others' rivalry, so neutrality alone could not guarantee safety.

53
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.389
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.457
Grammar focus: Concession (Although and Despite).

55
00:03:42.757 --> 00:03:44.072
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.783 --> 00:03:47.549
Despite the arms race, direct war was avoided.

57
00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.505
Although Cambodia tried to stay neutral, it was drawn into the war.

58
00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:56.391
In spite of détente, tensions remained high.

59
00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:08.591
'Although' + clause (subject + verb); 'despite / in spite of' + noun or -ing. Never 'despite of'. To use a clause after 'despite', add 'the fact that': 'Despite the fact that it was neutral, …'.

60
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:13.140
Be careful! Don't say: Despite of the arms race, …

61
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.576
Say: Despite the arms race, …

62
00:04:17.876 --> 00:04:19.469
'Despite' takes no 'of'.

63
00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:22.896
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.200
A Global Conflict.

65
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.203
The Cold War is often described as a standoff between two superpowers that never fought each other directly.

66
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:38.089
Despite this, the conflict was anything but cold for many countries in Asia, Africa and Latin America.

67
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:45.488
Although the USA and USSR avoided direct confrontation, they supported opposing sides in numerous proxy wars.

68
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:53.341
In Southeast Asia, Cold War rivalry contributed to decades of conflict in Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia.

69
00:04:54.333 --> 00:05:01.929
Historians increasingly argue that the Cold War must be understood from the perspective of these countries, not only from Washington and Moscow.

70
00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:06.849
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.683 --> 00:05:10.623
blank the danger, both sides kept building weapons.

72
00:05:10.923 --> 00:05:14.867
A, Although. B, Despite. C, Despite of.

73
00:05:18.433 --> 00:05:20.491
The answer is B: Despite.

74
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.393
blank it was neutral, the country was bombed.

75
00:05:24.693 --> 00:05:28.346
A, Although. B, Despite. C, In spite.

76
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:33.667
The answer is A: Although.

77
00:05:34.817 --> 00:05:38.534
A war where superpowers support opposing local sides is a blank.

78
00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:42.859
A, détente. B, proxy war. C, alliance.

79
00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.667
The answer is B: proxy war.

80
00:05:49.817 --> 00:05:52.362
A period of easing tension is called blank.

81
00:05:52.662 --> 00:05:56.965
A, détente. B, arms race. C, ideology.

82
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.767
The answer is A: détente.

83
00:06:03.917 --> 00:06:07.658
According to the reading, historians increasingly study the Cold War from…

84
00:06:07.958 --> 00:06:14.049
A, only Washington. B, the perspective of affected countries. C, Moscow alone.

85
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.958
The answer is B: the perspective of affected countries.

86
00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:25.167
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:26.017 --> 00:06:29.177
How did the Cold War affect countries outside Europe?

88
00:06:45.983 --> 00:06:47.495
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.795 --> 00:07:22.229
The Cold War hit countries outside Europe far harder than Europe itself. Although the superpowers never fought each other directly, they supported rival governments and rebel groups in Asia, Africa and Latin America. Cambodia, for example, was drawn into the Vietnam War despite its official neutrality, and heavy bombing helped destabilise the country. Local causes mattered too, but outside interference clearly made these conflicts longer and more destructive.

90
00:07:22.529 --> 00:07:24.099
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:24.983 --> 00:07:29.563
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concession (although and despite).

92
00:07:29.863 --> 00:07:35.653
Today's value is global citizenship. We all share one world. Help people near and far.

93
00:07:35.953 --> 00:07:38.663
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:38.963 --> 00:07:41.650
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

